      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2558.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 708,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and all known life, water is much more than just a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Unique States of Water\nWater is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** The flowing water we drink, swim in, and use for rain and oceans.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and hail.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air, clouds, and steam.\n\n### 2. Why Water is Magical (Sciencewise)\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it vital for the universe:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. Because of this, it acts as a carrier, dissolving and transporting vital nutrients, minerals, and chemicals in living organisms and rivers.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, **expands** and becomes *less dense* when it turns to ice. This is why ice cubes float in your glass—and crucially, why lakes freeze from the top down, allowing fish and underwater life to survive the winter.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why our bodies (which are mostly water) don’t overheat instantly.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other things (adhesion—which is how plants draw water up from their roots to their leaves).\n\n### 3. Water and Life\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n*   The human body is made up of about **60% water**. \n*   Our brains are roughly 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. \n*   It regulates our temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and transports oxygen through our blood.\n\n### 4. Water on Earth\n*   **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **The Scarcity:** Despite the Earth looking so blue, **96.5% of all Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater for human use (in lakes, rivers, and streams).\n\n### Summary\nWater is the ultimate substance of connection. It shapes our planet's geography, drives our weather systems, fuels our ecosystems, and runs through the veins of every living thing."
